# jade-status.expected — the recorded L3 status of the JADE port, per scenario.
#
# READ THE JUDGE'S DESIGN NOTE FIRST: .github/scripts/parity-junit.sh, the block
# above `jade-status)`. This file is only the data half of it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#
# `characterizationIT@jade` cannot honestly demand five green scenarios. #39
# measured the port against the frozen goldens over 20 runs per scenario per
# implementation, classified the residue **(b) normalizer gap, not closable**,
# and left three scenarios red (docs/parity-triage-jade.md). Those three do not
# fail because the port does the wrong thing -- the JADE run emits the SAME
# EVENT MULTISET as the 2008 application in all five scenarios, every run. They
# fail because the comparison is keyed on burst partition, burst partition is
# keyed on inter-message latency, and the goldens froze the baseline's latency.
#
# So this job asserts the MEASURED STATE rather than an aspiration, and it does
# it on the axis the measurement showed to be stable. Not the pass RATE -- three
# corpora read `capacity` 6/20, 15/20 and 12/12, and quoting any one of them as
# "the" rate would itself be misleading. The per-scenario VERDICT CLASS.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE VERDICT CLASSES (computed by parity-junit.sh from the failure anatomy)
#
#   MATCH      byte-identical to the golden.
#   ORDER      failed, SAME MULTISET -- the golden's events, a different burst
#              order. This is T-05 / T-06.
#   QUANTUM    failed, and the ONLY multiset difference is T-07: VOTE.diff
#              straddling the 1 000 ms `TIME_QUANTUM` bucket. Granted only when
#              every surplus line carries a `diff=<T~` and the two sides cancel.
#   BEHAVIOUR  failed with any other multiset difference -- a dropped or an
#              invented event. **Permitted nowhere in this file, and it is not
#              possible to permit it: the judge grants no scenario BEHAVIOUR.**
#   OTHER      not a golden comparison at all: an agent died, the run missed its
#              simulated-time bound, the harness threw. Permitted nowhere.
#   SKIP       assumed away. Permitted nowhere -- the whole point of this job is
#              that these five actually run. Before #40 they never had.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO READ A RED BUILD FROM THIS FILE
#
# A scenario that comes out anything other than its recorded status is red, IN
# EITHER DIRECTION. `opencybele-strict` does not list MATCH: it is 0/N in every
# corpus ever taken, so a `strict` that suddenly passes means the measurement
# below has gone stale, and that is as much a signal as a `lifecycle` that
# starts failing. Both mean somebody should look; neither should be edited away.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MEASUREMENT THIS PINS, AND WHAT RE-MEASURING TAKES
#
# Source: docs/parity-triage-jade.md §1 (three corpora) and §4.4 (the margins),
# plus PR #80's two measurement comments.
#
#   scenario     20-run corpus   2nd corpus   12-run idle   margin vs 220 ms
#   lifecycle    20/20           --           12/12         118 ms
#   timers       20/20           --           12/12         no gap in [120,400]
#   capacity      6/20           15/20        12/12           3 ms  (118 ms nearest)
#   congestion    3/20           --            7/12           9 ms
#   strict        0/20           0/20          0/12           0 ms
#
# The margins rank the pass rates exactly, on both implementations, and the
# small-margin rows swing by 2-4x between corpora while the large-margin ones do
# not move. `lifecycle` and `timers` are solid because they have margin, not
# because they are immune.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CPU-THROUGHPUT FLOOR, measured for #40 because "the CI runner is loaded"
# is a claim that deserves a number.
#
#   CPU budget (developer machine, 24 cores)   verdicts vs this file
#   24 cores, quiet                            green 3/3
#   4 dedicated cores (taskset -c 0-3), quiet  green 5/5
#   4 cores at ~50% steal                      red 3/3
#   24 cores at 100% steal                     red 4/4
#
# Below the floor the failure mode is TRUNCATION, not misordering: every
# scenario is bounded by an ABSOLUTE sim.stop.maxClockMs at pace 8, so a machine
# that cannot keep up with simulated time reaches the bound having emitted fewer
# events (strict 575 lines against 598, congestion 157 against 167). That
# surfaces as BEHAVIOUR -- which is what a port that dropped an event would look
# like too, and there is no fingerprint separating them.
#
# It is not a property of the port. Under the same starvation the DRIFT GUARD
# fails too: the frozen baseline stops matching its own goldens, 3/3 runs. The
# whole L3 lane has this floor, both arms, and it predates #40.
#
# So: if a CI run goes red with `HINT: the run is SHORT`, THE FIX IS NOT THIS
# FILE. It is that the runner is below the lane's throughput floor.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-measuring is NOT a CI action. It is ~20 runs per scenario on a quiet
# machine:
#
#   ./gradlew installDist
#   ./gradlew parityGate -Pjade.dist=$PWD/build/install/opencybele \
#             -Dparity.gate.runs=20 -Dparity.gate.scenario=opencybele-strict
#
# and the numbers above are what a proposed edit to this file has to argue
# against. Editing a row to make a build green, without a corpus behind it, is
# the failure this whole mechanism exists to prevent.
#
# Format: <scenario> <permitted verdict>...   (whitespace separated, # comments)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Solid across every corpus: 32/32 combined, and 118 ms of margin. These two
# must pass. A failure here is a regression, full stop.
opencybele-lifecycle    MATCH
opencybele-timers       MATCH

# Load-sensitive. The rate swings 6/20 -> 15/20 -> 12/12 and 3/20 -> 7/12 with
# machine load, so a single CI run on a loaded shared runner cannot distinguish
# "passed" from "failed" as a signal, and pinning either one would be pinning
# the runner rather than the program. What IS pinned is that when they fail,
# they fail as an ORDERING difference and never as a behaviour difference --
# which is the claim every corpus supports.
opencybele-capacity     MATCH ORDER
opencybele-congestion   MATCH ORDER

# 0/N in every corpus taken, on either machine state -- deterministic, not
# flaky, and the same boundary fails the BASELINE 3/20 under concurrent load, at
# the same line and in the same direction (§4.2). MATCH is deliberately absent:
# `strict` passing is a status change and goes red.
#
# QUANTUM is here because #39 measured T-07 on this scenario specifically:
# VOTE.diff for (vl8, stH) is 3214..3254 on the baseline and 2809..3116 on the
# port, so it quantises to <T~3000> every time on one and straddles the bucket
# on the other -- 10 runs each way in a corpus of 20.
opencybele-strict       ORDER QUANTUM
